{
 "note": "The world's best-known parks, as a demand list to check our coverage against. Written 2026-09-18 on Hidde's ask: \"zou je een onderzoek kunnen doen naar de bekendste parken in de wereld waar het meest voor wordt gezocht op Google. En of we die al in onze database hebben.\" Read by scripts/park_demand.py, which crosses each one against our published trees BY DISTANCE (never by name, per CLAUDE.md) and says what we have. A park earns a Contract H page at five mapped trees plus a written intro.",
 "coordinates": "Centroids, and they are APPROXIMATE: written from knowledge, not fetched, because this session's network policy blocks every Wikimedia host. They are used only to ask which of our trees stand near a park, with a per-park radius, so a few hundred metres of error changes nothing. `python3 scripts/park_demand.py --coords` replaces them with Wikipedia's own coordinates on any machine that can reach the API, and prints what moved.",
 "evidence": "google maps ... = Google's own Year in Search ranking of the most searched parks on Google Maps, which is the closest thing to search volume anybody publishes for free. visitors/yr = press-reported footfall. hand-seeded = on the list because it is world-famous, with fame still to be measured by --views (Wikipedia pageviews, the same proxy demand.py uses for cities).",
 "out_of_focus": "Kirstenbosch, Gorky Park and HaYarkon Park sit in regions Hidde ruled out of focus on 2026-08-16, and Cubbon Park is in India. They stay listed so the world list is honest and the script skips them, exactly as scout_next.py does. The regions come from data/register-scouting.json, never from a second copy here.",
